'''''Tradescantia zanonia''''', formerly known as '''''Campelia zanonia''''', is an evergreen perennial subshrub of the [[dayflower family]], closely related to ''[[Tradescantia zebrina]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Burns |first=Jean H. |last2=Faden |first2=Robert B. |last3=Steppan |first3=Scott J. |date=2011-06-01 |title=Phylogenetic studies in the Commelinaceae subfamily Commelinoideae inferred from nuclear ribosomal and chloroplast DNA sequences |url=https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1600/036364411X569471 |journal=Systematic Botany |volume=36 |issue=2 |pages=268–276 |doi=10.1600/036364411X569471 |issn=0363-6445|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hertweck |first=Kate L. |last2=Pires |first2=J. Chris |date=2014-03-01 |title=Systematics and evolution of inflorescence structure in the ''Tradescantia'' alliance (Commelinaceae) |url=https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/aspt/sb/2014/00000039/00000001/art00011;jsessionid=2ng7evtk646lj.x-ic-live-01 |journal=Systematic Botany |volume=39 |issue=1 |pages=105–116 |doi=10.1600/036364414X677991|url-access=subscription }}</ref> It is native to the tropical Americas, ranging from Mexico through Central America and the Caribbean to Bolivia and southern Brazil.<ref name="powo" /> Its common names include ''cañagria, cascajo flor, cinta, rascadera'', and ''suelda''.<ref name = powo/>

Its [[Capsule (fruit)|capsule]] is covered by the fleshy purple-black [[calyx (botany)|calyx]], hence a berry-like appearance.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hunt |first=D. R. |date=1986 |title=''Campelia'', ''Rhoeo'' and ''Zebrina'' united with ''Tradescantia'': American Commelinaceae: XIII |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/4102948 |journal=Kew Bulletin |volume=41 |issue=2 |pages=401–405 |doi=10.2307/4102948 |issn=0075-5974|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

It is cultivated as a greenhouse ornamental. There is a variegated cultivar named 'Mexican Flag'.

